lesson: 138
title: The unknown number: equations as stories
level: primary-2
stars: 2
explain: An equation is a story with a missing number. «A number times 3, plus 5, is 26» is written □ × 3 + 5 = 26.
explain: Undo the steps from the end: take away the 5 to get 21, then divide by 3 to get 7.
explain: Check: 7 × 3 + 5 = 26. On a balance, 3 equal bags and a 5 kg weight against 26 kg mean the bags weigh 21 kg, so one bag weighs 7 kg.
vars: a=name k=2..5 v=2..12 b=2..20
let: c = k * v + b
answer: v
exercise: {a} thinks of a number. {a.He} multiplies it by {k} and adds {b}. The result is {c}. What number did {a.he} think of?
exercise: On one pan of a balance there are {k} equal bags of sugar and a {b} kg weight. On the other pan there is {c} kg. How many kilograms does one bag weigh?
exercise: {a} bought {k} notebooks at the same price and a pen for {b} dollars. {a.He} paid {c} dollars. How much did one notebook cost?
exercise: {k} boxes with the same number of eggs and {b} loose eggs make {c} eggs. How many eggs are in a box?
exercise: Find the number: {k} times the number, plus {b}, makes {c}.
exercise: {a} had {b} stickers. After getting {k} packs of stickers, all the same, {a.he} has {c}. How many stickers are in a pack?
test: A mystery number is taken {k} times and then {b} is added, giving {c}. What is the mystery number?
test: {c} kg is the weight of a {b} kg crate together with {k} melons of equal weight inside it. How heavy is one melon, in kilograms?
test: For {c} dollars {a} got a {b}-dollar ball and {k} identical toy cars. What did each car cost?
test: {a} has {c} marbles: {b} in {a.his} pocket and the rest in {k} bags holding equal numbers. How many marbles does each bag hold?
exercise: What number did {a} think of, if {a.he} multiplied it by {k}, added {b} and got {c}?
exercise: A number × {k} + {b} = {c}. Find the number.
exercise: {a} bought {k} comics at the same price and a bookmark for {b} dollars in a shop in Chicago. {a.He} spent {c} dollars. What did one comic cost?
exercise: On a scale, {k.words} equal bags of flour and a {b.words}-kilogram sack weigh {c} kilograms in all. How many kilograms does one bag weigh?
exercise: {a} had {b} cards. Then {a.he} got {k} packs with the same number of cards in each and now has {c} cards. How many cards are in one pack?
exercise: In a bakery in Melbourne there are {c} buns: {b} on the counter and the rest on {k} trays holding the same number. How many buns are on each tray?
exercise: Multiply a number by {k}, then add {b}: the answer is {c}. What is the number?
exercise: A minibus in Ontario carries {c} people: {b} adults and {k} classes of children of the same size. How many children are in one class?
test: {c} dollars paid for {k} tickets of equal price and a {b}-dollar programme at a theatre in London. How much was one ticket?
test: If {k} times a secret number, increased by {b}, gives {c}, what is the secret number?
test: A box of {k} identical jars and {b} kg of packing weighs {c} kg. What is the weight of one jar, in kilograms?
test: {a} in Dublin counted {c} shells: {b} loose ones and {k} full buckets with equal numbers. How many shells are in a bucket?
test: Together, {k} equal stacks of plates and {b} single plates make {c} plates. How many plates are in one stack?
test: {a} spent {c} dollars at a fair in Iowa: {b} dollars for a hot dog and the rest on {k} rides that all cost the same. What did one ride cost?
exercise: {a} thinks of a number, times it by {k}, adds {b}, gets {c}. whats the number
exercise: {a} bought {k} notebooks same price + a pen for {b} dollars, paid {c+1} — no wait, {c} dollars. how much one notebook
exercise: this kid in my class says if {k} times a number plus {b} is {c} then the number's {c-b}. nah. what is it really
exercise: balance: {k} same bags of sugar and a {b} kg weight on one side, {c} kg on the other, the shop's 2 km away btw. how heavy is one bag in kg
test: i picked a number, x{k}, then +{b}, got {c}. what did i pick
test: {k} same tickets and a {b} dollar snack cost {c} dollars. one ticket how much
test: {a} reckons: number times {k}, plus {b}, makes {c}, so the number's {v+b}. thats wrong innit? whats the number
exam: It is given that a certain number, upon multiplication by {k} and the subsequent addition of {b}, yields {c}. Determine the said number.
exam: Whereas one pan of a balance bears {k} identical bags of sugar together with a weight of {b} kg, and the other pan bears {c} kg, the balance being in equilibrium, ascertain the mass in kilograms of a single bag.
exam: {a} purchased {k} notebooks at a uniform price together with a pen priced at {b} dollars, the total expenditure being {c} dollars. State the price of one notebook in dollars.
exam: Compute the value of the unknown, given that {k} times the unknown, increased by {b}, is equal to {c}.
exercise: Grandad passed away and left a jar of {c} old coins. {b} coins went to the animal shelter he loved, and the rest were shared equally between his {k} grandchildren. How many coins did each grandchild get?
exercise: When {a}'s mum was made redundant, she was paid {c} dollars in all: a one-off {b} dollars plus {k} equal weekly payments. How many dollars was one weekly payment?
test: tin had {c} biscuits, now there's bloody {b} left. {k} of 'em scoffed the rest, same each, and {a} swears {a.he} "only had a couple" — creative with the truth much? how many did {a.he} really eat
